    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-54202",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cfc862ea-961c-50e4-a89b-10c5ef88d851",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle Given Its Equation",
      "problem": "What is the center and radius of the circle with the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=169?$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=169$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =169 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\ r & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and its radius is $13.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
