      "knowledge_point_id": "e75012ec-8203-574b-ba07-0a26e4cf4691",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given Endpoints of a Diameter",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/assets/883828970a6deca7d7efdc201da679324f17ec916ba0b44b76f5af2718ead2b9.png)\n\nGiven that the points $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(3,5)$ and $(-3,-5),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{{(5-(-5))}^{2}+{(3-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 34} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{2}=\\sqrt{34}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{34})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
